Here is a program (fortran) to print out a woman in a kimono.  I got this
in Japan. I didn't write it, but the name of the author and the school
he was going to is in the program and data file respectively.  Run this
shell script through sh.  i.e. save to kimono.sh or whatever and type
sh kimono.sh.

John Blankenagel

---------cut-here-------------------------------------------------
#
echo 'extracting README'
cat <<'//== E*O*F README ==//' >README
I got this program when I was in Japan a few years ago.  I helped 
some students at a community college debug their Othelo program and they
gave me this program on a paper tape (what? you never heard of paper tape?).
I loaded it into our Gould, fixed it and this is the result.  You need
a program which converts its output to a form that your printer can use.
I.e. a program which converts fortran output stuff to printer stuff.  Our
computer has a program called asa.  You just type the following:

     kimono < kimono.dat | asa | lpr

and it will print it.

//== E*O*F README ==//
#
#
echo 'extracting Makefile'
cat <<'//== E*O*F Makefile ==//' >Makefile
all: kimono.o
	f77 kimono.o -o kimono
//== E*O*F Makefile ==//
